# Word Template Setup

## Overview

The Word template contains four Content Controls that Power Automate fills when the flow runs. The template defines all formatting — fonts, spacing, headers, footers, page layout. The Content Controls are replaced with plain text at runtime.

---

## Why Content Controls (Not Text Placeholders)

The Power Automate `Populate a Word template` action exclusively recognizes native Word Content Controls. Text placeholders in any format — `{{REPORT_DATE}}`, `[REPORT_DATE]`, `<REPORT_DATE>` — are not recognized. Attempting to use text placeholders causes this error:

> "The selected file doesn't contain template elements"

Content Controls must be inserted manually using the Developer tab in Word desktop. Word Online does not reliably save Content Controls.

---

## Required Content Controls

| Control Name | Location in Document | Content Type |
|---|---|---|
| `REPORT_DATE` | Title block (appears twice — title and Section 1 header) | Plain Text |
| `EXECUTIVE_SUMMARY` | Section 1 body | Plain Text |
| `SITE_SUMMARIES` | Section 2 body | Plain Text |
| `APPENDIX_CONTENT` | Section 3 body | Plain Text |

---

## Step-by-Step: Adding Content Controls

### Step 1 — Enable the Developer Tab

1. Open the template in **Word desktop** (not Word Online)
2. Go to **File → Options → Customize Ribbon**
3. In the right column, check **Developer**
4. Click **OK**

The Developer tab now appears in the ribbon.

### Step 2 — Insert a Content Control

For each of the four locations in the template:

1. Place your cursor at the location where the content should appear (or select any existing placeholder text)
2. Go to the **Developer tab**
3. In the **Controls** group, click **Plain Text Content Control** (the `Aa` icon)
4. A grey box appears at the cursor location — this is the Content Control

### Step 3 — Name the Content Control

With the Content Control selected:

1. Click **Properties** in the Controls group
2. In the **Title** field, enter the exact name (e.g. `REPORT_DATE`)
3. In the **Tag** field, enter the same name
4. Click **OK**

Repeat for all four Content Controls.

### Step 4 — Save and Upload

1. Save the file as `.docx`
2. Upload it to the SharePoint library designated for templates
3. In the Power Automate `Populate a Word template` step, reselect this file — the four Content Controls will appear as mappable fields

